body:
**Shape.** A local declared without an initializer adopts the owned state from its first assignment (§28.3.3 *Adoption*); its second assignment must dispose the displaced value (K3.11, §28.7). It does not.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class Q
  private _id: int;
  public constructor(id: int)
    _id := id;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
end Q;

public sealed class R
  private _id: int;
  public owned f: Q;
  public constructor(id: int, owned q: Q)
    _id := id;
    f := q;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
  public static method Take(owned r: R)
    using var k := move r;
  end Take;
  public static method Body()
    var b: R;                         // uninitialized, adopts the first assignment
    b := new R(2, new Q(1));
    b := new R(4, new Q(3));          // drop-on-overwrite must dispose R#2/Q#1 here; nothing is disposed
  end Body;
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(): int
    R.Body();
    Console.WriteLine("END");
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35.** Zero diagnostics (the K3.14 must-use lint aside), both modes print `D:4 D:3 END`, exit 0: `R#2`/`Q#1` are never disposed. The same program with `var b := new R(2, new Q(1)); b := new R(4, new Q(3));` prints `D:2 D:1 D:4 D:3 END` as required, so the defect is keyed on the declaration form, not on the overwrite itself. (Two typed-declaration locals: `var a: R; var c: R; c := new R(); c := new R();` leaks the same way.)

**Spec (v0.345).** §28.3.3 *Adoption*: *"The adopted state is that of the first assignment that reaches the local on each path — owned from a fresh producer"*; *State match on reassignment* rule 1: an owned-state LHS *"disposes the displaced value per drop-on-overwrite (§28.7)"*; K3.11: *"reassignment of an owned location — a consume of the displaced value … after the RHS is evaluated (RHS-first)"*. Expected trace: `D:2 D:1` at the second assignment, then `D:4 D:3` at scope end.

**Differential numbers.** 23 of 6,000 sampled programs (fields and try/while samples) are level-2 trace disagreements of exactly this shape (`decl x / asg x / asg x` with any statements around); every other accepted program in those samples traces identically on both sides.

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
